<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giovinezza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">composed <a href="/years/1909">1909</a>; March on Rome version <a href="/years/1921">1921</a>; fascist words <a href="/years/1924">1924</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Giovinezza&quot; (&quot;Youth&quot;) was composed by the lawyer and composer Giuseppe Blanc in 1909 as &quot;Il Commiato&quot; (&quot;Farewell&quot;), a graduation song of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-turin">University of Turin</a>. It became popular among Italian soldiers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and as the &quot;Inno degli Arditi&quot; it was the hymn of the Arditi, an assault corps of the Royal Army whose members later joined the fascist movement in large numbers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With &quot;<b>Giovinezza</b>&quot;, it remained the best-known song of the Fascist years.</blockquote><a href="/songs/faccetta-nera">Faccetta nera</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Giovinezza</b>&quot; (&quot;Youth&quot;) was composed by the lawyer and composer Giuseppe Blanc in 1909 as &quot;Il Commiato&quot; (&quot;Farewell&quot;), a graduation song of the University of Turin.</blockquote><a href="/songs/giovinezza">Giovinezza</a></li></ul></section>
